    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d462">

                <pb facs="0612" n="600" xml:id="pg600"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.61/950: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The People’s Commissar for Foreign Affairs of the Soviet
                        Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Molotov</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Translation]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi>
                        </placeName> [<date ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content"
                            calendar="gregorian" notAfter="1943-11-17T04:01:00-04:00"
                            notBefore="1943-11-16T00:00:00+03:00">undated</date>].<lb/>[Received
                        November 17, 1943—4:01 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The decade which has elapsed since the establishment of Soviet-American
                    diplomatic relations<anchor n="33" xml:id="d462-f1"/> has demonstrated that the
                    relations between our countries are based on a strong foundation and that they
                    completely correspond to the interests of our peoples. The common struggle
                    against Hitlerite Germany has even more firmly cemented our relations and
                    furthered the growth of mutual understanding and confidence between our
                    countries and the development of Soviet-American military cooperation. The
                    recent Moscow Conference has demonstrated that Soviet-American relations possess
                    all the elements to develop successfully the conditions necessary for shortening
                    the period of the war and for the establishment of a durable and long peace in
                    the interests of all freedom-loving peoples.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">V. Molotov</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d462-f1" n="33" xml:id="d462-f1-content">
                <p>For correspondence concerning the negotiations and agreements by which relations
                    were established between the United States and the Soviet Union, see <ref
                        target="frus1933-39#pg_1">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, The Soviet Union, 1933–1939,
                        pp. 1</ref> ff.</p>
                <p>For a telegram sent on the occasion of this tenth anniversary by Secretary Hull
                    to Mr. Molotov, dated November 15, 1943, see Department of State <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>, November 20, 1943, p. 373.</p>
                <p>For this occasion the newspaper <hi rendition="#italic">Izvestiya</hi> on
                    November 16 published a cordial, leading article on the “strengthening of
                    Soviet-American friendship”. A two-polumn front page editorial appeared in <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Pravda</hi> on the following day.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
